{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-330650",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/967a47f879a672d2b66c9ba1bac8ae3ddea220212ced411ccf382301f3c4b56e.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>-a)=P(Z<a)=\u03a6(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z<1.63).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/11598fe8ebdf6e04b11bc1a41a887378c1dfa8a5136b970328411325eccb5a50.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z>-1.63).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/de76fcee7abfd8b2374c2ddc5f038114181ac60fcdbed08ad335f15d196c79a0.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<1.63) & =P(Z>-1.63) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-1.63) \\\\  & =1-\u03a6(-1.63) \\\\  & =1-0.0516 \\\\  & =0.9484.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
